1
00:00:18,065 --> 00:00:28,307
[SPEAKER_00]: The guided meditation, it should be, or meditation in general, be it guided or not, it should be about understanding.

2
00:00:28,327 --> 00:00:33,648
[SPEAKER_00]: A person should not be looking for a special novelty experience to come out of it.

3
00:00:35,229 --> 00:00:40,530
[SPEAKER_00]: It should look for clarity and understanding.

4
00:00:40,550 --> 00:00:46,231
[SPEAKER_00]: They will then obviously, if done rightly, result in freedom from suffering and peace.

5
00:00:49,845 --> 00:00:58,850
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether a person is just meditating for 10-15 minutes or dedicating 5 hours a day, it doesn't matter.

6
00:00:59,691 --> 00:01:10,017
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is that that time is spent towards establishing the mind in clarity of experience, of the nature of experience.

7
00:01:10,957 --> 00:01:13,899
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the instruction is necessary.

8
00:01:14,159 --> 00:01:19,262
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know what to look for, if you don't know what to discern,

9
00:01:21,243 --> 00:01:22,444
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't find it by yourself.

10
00:01:24,605 --> 00:01:35,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even as the guided meditation goes, it should be guiding towards understanding, as you often read about it in the suttas.

11
00:01:39,836 --> 00:01:46,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say that a person does want to settle their mind.

12
00:01:49,258 --> 00:01:56,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Without falling into sort of mystical absorptions, but without over-rationalizing either.

13
00:01:58,185 --> 00:02:03,449
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first thing would be solitude, physical solitude.

14
00:02:04,910 --> 00:02:06,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Physically removed from other people.

15
00:02:09,854 --> 00:02:16,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Far enough to not hear them, far enough to not see them, far enough to not feel that you can be encroached by them at any given time.

16
00:02:18,030 --> 00:02:19,330
[SPEAKER_00]: So, secluded and remote.

17
00:02:27,553 --> 00:02:40,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you're secluded and remote, ask yourself, what is really, what is the goal of the practice?

18
00:02:40,996 --> 00:02:45,737
[SPEAKER_00]: What is, what is my specific purpose?

19
00:02:46,418 --> 00:02:47,538
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I trying to achieve?

20
00:02:51,865 --> 00:02:52,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I after happiness?

21
00:02:53,265 --> 00:02:55,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I after peace?

22
00:02:56,086 --> 00:02:58,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I after freedom from suffering?

23
00:02:58,926 --> 00:03:03,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Are all these things sort of merged into one one goal that I want to achieve?

24
00:03:15,129 --> 00:03:20,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever the answer may be, it should be clear that the answer is not about

25
00:03:25,581 --> 00:03:35,926
[SPEAKER_00]: That experience being given to you, the answer is about that experience being developed, the experience of peace, happiness, freedom from suffering and so on.

26
00:03:36,987 --> 00:03:40,528
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you develop it by knowing where you're supposed to develop.

27
00:03:41,909 --> 00:03:49,493
[SPEAKER_00]: It's what Buddha said, he measured... he goes and listens to the wise people, a man goes in with the wise people, then he reflects upon what he has said, that he measures the task.

28
00:03:51,013 --> 00:03:54,015
[SPEAKER_00]: So, measuring the task means knowing what you need to do.

29
00:03:58,251 --> 00:04:05,499
[SPEAKER_00]: And all the tools for freedom from suffering are already contained within your experience as a whole, within the five aggregates.

30
00:04:07,321 --> 00:04:10,104
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, the reason for suffering is craving.

31
00:04:11,823 --> 00:04:14,825
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason for freedom from suffering is the extinguishing of craving.

32
00:04:15,065 --> 00:04:16,906
[SPEAKER_00]: And craving is not to be found elsewhere.

33
00:04:17,366 --> 00:04:18,747
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in things you crave for.

34
00:04:19,247 --> 00:04:20,648
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in things you're attached to.

35
00:04:21,328 --> 00:04:24,050
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in things that you find dear and pleasing.

36
00:04:24,070 --> 00:04:24,970
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in the world.

37
00:04:25,090 --> 00:04:26,551
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in the object of your senses.

38
00:04:26,611 --> 00:04:28,292
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in the object of your attention.

39
00:04:29,072 --> 00:04:33,555
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in your attitude towards things that you can attend, perceive and engage with.

40
00:04:38,077 --> 00:04:41,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were to remove the craving gradually,

41
00:04:43,008 --> 00:04:51,532
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to stop feeding it, if you were to allow it to fade away through non-maintenance, then the result is freedom from suffering.

42
00:04:51,812 --> 00:04:54,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, within this very present experience as a whole.

43
00:04:55,653 --> 00:04:57,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Not before or after, right now.

44
00:05:06,938 --> 00:05:10,460
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having gone to a secluded place, away from people,

45
00:05:12,083 --> 00:05:14,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Probably takes some time for a mind to settle in into that solitude.

46
00:05:18,929 --> 00:05:31,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever that is, without overdoing it, without overattending it, just start regarding yourself as part of that non-inhabited setup.

47
00:05:33,141 --> 00:05:39,947
[SPEAKER_00]: You are part of that remote forest, cave, root of a tree, a hut, wherever you might be.

48
00:05:41,382 --> 00:05:49,404
[SPEAKER_00]: There are trees, there are rocks, there are rivers, there are waterfalls, there is your body there as well.

49
00:05:50,344 --> 00:05:54,826
[SPEAKER_00]: There are monkeys, there are birds, there are snakes, there is your body there as well.

50
00:05:56,166 --> 00:05:58,427
[SPEAKER_00]: You are not separate from it.

51
00:05:58,467 --> 00:06:07,409
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason why it's important to start reflecting like this, because the whole point of uprooting the craving,

52
00:06:08,181 --> 00:06:11,305
[SPEAKER_00]: is through that non-maintenance, as I just mentioned.

53
00:06:12,847 --> 00:06:23,742
[SPEAKER_00]: And that non-maintenance is developed and discerned when you realize that you can't maintain it, even if you want to.

54
00:06:25,172 --> 00:06:31,436
[SPEAKER_00]: When you realize that the very foundation of your own life is not in your own control.

55
00:06:32,296 --> 00:06:33,677
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no say in it.

56
00:06:34,578 --> 00:06:37,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Your life is a secondary by-product of it.

57
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:40,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Your choices, decisions you've been making are secondary to it.

58
00:06:42,903 --> 00:06:46,565
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's nothing other than impermanence that the Buddha was talking about.

59
00:06:47,646 --> 00:06:49,347
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you reflecting,

60
00:06:51,278 --> 00:07:13,494
[SPEAKER_00]: That in your solitary setup, like in this case, this cave here, this cave and huge tree trunks, broken trees, broken rocks, split side of the mountains and so on, your body is actually closer to those things than it is closer to your point of view.

61
00:07:15,922 --> 00:07:21,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Your body belongs to these rocks and trees and forests, mountains and so on.

62
00:07:22,066 --> 00:07:25,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Belongs, as in, it's made of the same elements.

63
00:07:25,288 --> 00:07:30,951
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole your, of your body, as in my, it's completely gratuitous.

64
00:07:32,252 --> 00:07:38,216
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason you can regard your body as yours is because there is something to be regarded as mine.

65
00:07:38,316 --> 00:07:41,478
[SPEAKER_00]: And that something precedes your ownership.

66
00:07:42,648 --> 00:07:46,329
[SPEAKER_00]: That something precedes your your appropriation of this is mine.

67
00:07:46,969 --> 00:07:48,849
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you're reminding yourself of.

68
00:07:49,189 --> 00:07:54,530
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you're, well, maybe reminding yourself or discerning it for the first time.

69
00:07:54,811 --> 00:07:55,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

70
00:07:56,031 --> 00:08:10,014
[SPEAKER_00]: The solitary setup, free from people, free from disturbances, where you are alone, left on your own devices, you start to see that your own point of view is actually

71
00:08:12,348 --> 00:08:16,510
[SPEAKER_00]: It's almost, almost incidental in a grand scheme of things.

72
00:08:16,550 --> 00:08:24,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Grand scheme of things of the entire four great elements.

73
00:08:26,333 --> 00:08:28,094
[SPEAKER_00]: The mountains, the forest that you are in.

74
00:08:28,834 --> 00:08:31,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Your body shares that nature.

75
00:08:31,455 --> 00:08:32,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Your body is that.

76
00:08:34,156 --> 00:08:39,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, as these great rocks, I mean, they're huge.

77
00:08:40,401 --> 00:08:46,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you can look and see, even they break down, split and crumble.

78
00:08:48,788 --> 00:09:02,759
[SPEAKER_00]: And what then is to be said about this human body of quite a soft, soft form, soft composition, mild in comparison to these granite mountains.

79
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:06,963
[SPEAKER_00]: But if the granite mountains, even they cannot escape,

80
00:09:08,935 --> 00:09:10,055
[SPEAKER_00]: The gradual destruction.

81
00:09:10,115 --> 00:09:12,576
[SPEAKER_00]: What is to be said about this very soft body?

82
00:09:15,637 --> 00:09:22,438
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is not for the purpose of some kind of poetical musings or romantic notions.

83
00:09:22,519 --> 00:09:22,659
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

84
00:09:22,839 --> 00:09:30,941
[SPEAKER_00]: It's undermining your own very sense of existence, which is basically your own very sense of control, mastery and ownership.

85
00:09:31,501 --> 00:09:36,342
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not necessarily going to be pleasant, especially not if it's discerned for the first time.

86
00:09:38,195 --> 00:09:47,686
[SPEAKER_00]: These things are frightening for those who still want to hold on to their appropriated point of view as the owner of the body and so on.

87
00:09:48,967 --> 00:09:53,172
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people would sometimes freak out in the suttas when they would start to comprehend this.

88
00:09:55,054 --> 00:09:58,197
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you learn how to find peace in it.

89
00:09:59,212 --> 00:10:00,113
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not really optional.

90
00:10:00,553 --> 00:10:01,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear is optional.

91
00:10:01,594 --> 00:10:02,554
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to fear it.

92
00:10:03,115 --> 00:10:09,539
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will automatically fear it, if it contradicts your point of view of ownership, which you're refusing to let go of.

93
00:10:10,539 --> 00:10:17,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're gradually undermining your own sense of ownership, whereby, well, even the fear cannot be owned.

94
00:10:17,364 --> 00:10:22,227
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you stop being afraid, but you do have to accept, you do have to go into that direction.

95
00:10:22,947 --> 00:10:26,710
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this very body, six feet tall,

96
00:10:29,923 --> 00:10:33,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Surrounded by wilderness belongs to the wilderness.

97
00:10:33,968 --> 00:10:35,629
[SPEAKER_00]: It belongs to the great planet Earth.

98
00:10:37,131 --> 00:10:50,446
[SPEAKER_00]: It belongs to a complete set of its own laws and circumstances and accidents and change of elements and so on, which means not even a tiny bit, tiny grain of this

99
00:10:52,070 --> 00:10:55,391
[SPEAKER_00]: The human body can be mine, actually, can obey me.

100
00:10:55,791 --> 00:11:07,635
[SPEAKER_00]: The beating heart, the muscle that pumps the blood completely involuntarily, is like these rivers flowing here, the trees growing and so on.

101
00:11:08,035 --> 00:11:12,256
[SPEAKER_00]: But if something little changes, those great things will be gone.

102
00:11:12,496 --> 00:11:17,038
[SPEAKER_00]: What is to say for a little inferior tiny muscle in your chest that pumps

103
00:11:17,933 --> 00:11:22,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, that's why the Buddha would talk about these things in the suttas.

104
00:11:22,097 --> 00:11:25,260
[SPEAKER_00]: You are to reflect on it, so that you actually feel as not in control.

105
00:11:44,015 --> 00:11:46,916
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the virtue needs to be established beforehand.

106
00:11:47,296 --> 00:11:59,900
[SPEAKER_00]: You must have been dwelling restrained and keeping the precepts, because if you start undermining your own sense of self, your own point of view, which is all about control and mastery, well, you start undermining your sanity very often.

107
00:12:00,120 --> 00:12:05,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Not always, but very often, because that fear can overwhelm, as it happened many times in the suttas.

108
00:12:06,943 --> 00:12:12,905
[SPEAKER_00]: But it can't overwhelm you if your behavior has been purified beforehand, as the Buddha would say.

109
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:39,216
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by dwelling in the solitary environment, free from others, you actually get to see that whatever personality, whatever sense of self you have created in the course of this life, it's fully dependent upon this living, operating organs of this body that are on the level of trees, rocks, rivers, mountains, and so on.

110
00:12:40,958 --> 00:12:42,459
[SPEAKER_00]: And when those things come to cease,

111
00:12:43,758 --> 00:12:45,719
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these organs will also come to see.

112
00:12:45,760 --> 00:12:50,403
[SPEAKER_00]: They cannot possibly remain standing.

113
00:12:50,523 --> 00:12:56,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a question of looking for a great epiphany or some great revelation of the hidden truth.

114
00:12:57,228 --> 00:13:03,452
[SPEAKER_00]: The question is of clarifying this very image, this very reflection, maintaining it and letting it endure.

115
00:13:04,373 --> 00:13:06,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Letting it exist peripherally while you go and do other things.

116
00:13:07,155 --> 00:13:08,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Establish your mind in it.

117
00:13:09,325 --> 00:13:12,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're basically establishing your mind in non-appropriation.

118
00:13:12,926 --> 00:13:16,167
[SPEAKER_00]: You're establishing your mind in the impossibility of appropriation.

119
00:13:17,747 --> 00:13:18,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it doesn't...

120
00:13:18,768 --> 00:13:22,308
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you don't think twice about these branches and stones in here.

121
00:13:22,348 --> 00:13:23,949
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody were to ask you, is this yours?

122
00:13:24,009 --> 00:13:24,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Does this belong to you?

123
00:13:25,269 --> 00:13:26,590
[SPEAKER_00]: The answer is no, of course not.

124
00:13:27,770 --> 00:13:32,431
[SPEAKER_00]: But if somebody were to ask you about your own body, you'd say, well, it is my body.

125
00:13:32,911 --> 00:13:34,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you might stop and think about it.

126
00:13:34,732 --> 00:13:35,232
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

127
00:13:36,217 --> 00:13:48,530
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same manner, these objects around you in this wilderness, accidental forest, that's also your body, shares the same nature.

128
00:13:50,272 --> 00:13:57,520
[SPEAKER_00]: None of this is yours and that body cannot be yours, because if it were yours, you would be in charge of your own heart.

129
00:13:58,758 --> 00:14:00,379
[SPEAKER_00]: The way it beats, you could tell it to stop.

130
00:14:01,159 --> 00:14:08,322
[SPEAKER_00]: You would be in charge of your own metabolism, your own lungs, brain, and so on.

131
00:14:08,422 --> 00:14:08,963
[SPEAKER_00]: But you aren't.

132
00:14:09,903 --> 00:14:13,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Any notion of control, intentionality, and choice that comes to you, it's secondary.

133
00:14:13,865 --> 00:14:17,306
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fully within the container of this given body.

134
00:14:20,148 --> 00:14:27,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, when that given body ceases to be given to you, all the things that you do on account of it,

135
00:14:28,877 --> 00:14:30,579
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not be able to stand anymore.

136
00:14:35,944 --> 00:14:48,537
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seclusion, in order to stop identifying yourself with people, with external sense of control, with external sense of management, but instead,

137
00:14:50,028 --> 00:15:00,899
[SPEAKER_00]: As stepping back into something greater, something more accidental, something that's... that cannot be conceived to be controlled, such as wilderness.

138
00:15:08,507 --> 00:15:12,832
[SPEAKER_00]: And then maintaining that, not expecting a great

139
00:15:14,912 --> 00:15:16,973
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I meant by non-maintenance of it.

140
00:15:38,392 --> 00:15:40,153
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't stop maintaining it overnight.

141
00:15:40,894 --> 00:15:48,660
[SPEAKER_00]: But you set up the environment that prevents, that cannot give rise to maintenance of craving of one's own appropriation.

142
00:15:49,181 --> 00:15:52,063
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how craving bleeds out, fades away.

143
00:15:54,085 --> 00:16:00,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, even if you're with people afterwards, there will not be the induction, re-induction of your own ignorance and your own appropriation.

144
00:16:04,511 --> 00:16:13,456
[SPEAKER_00]: So, giant roots, massive rocks, huge banyan trees, great mountains, large rivers.

145
00:16:13,516 --> 00:16:15,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, all that is accidental.

146
00:16:16,678 --> 00:16:21,922
[SPEAKER_00]: What is to be said about the blood flowing in this body, the heart pumping, the lungs breathing?

147
00:16:23,823 --> 00:16:27,185
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, probably even these small trees and branches are more durable.

148
00:16:28,409 --> 00:16:29,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, this body is.

149
00:16:29,629 --> 00:16:36,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, somehow, this body is regarded as mine, as in my... as it is in my control.

150
00:16:37,831 --> 00:16:39,131
[SPEAKER_00]: As if it is in my control.

151
00:16:41,932 --> 00:16:44,712
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about denying your sense of control.

152
00:16:46,053 --> 00:16:49,093
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about undermining it while it is still present.

153
00:16:49,373 --> 00:16:56,955
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is me, there is this sense of I am, but you want to undermine it by seeing it fully determined by that which cannot be.

154
00:16:58,042 --> 00:16:58,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Yours.

155
00:16:59,623 --> 00:17:02,304
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how your own sense of control ceases to be yours.

156
00:17:02,825 --> 00:17:09,749
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how arahant can still act, talk, choose, prefer, without actually owning anything.

157
00:17:09,769 --> 00:17:12,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he has fully surpassed it, surmounted it.

158
00:17:14,111 --> 00:17:20,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only reason people appropriate things is because they don't fully understand that they cannot do so.

159
00:17:21,835 --> 00:17:25,256
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I can say, yes, this mountain is mine, but it's completely meaningless.

160
00:17:25,937 --> 00:17:27,097
[SPEAKER_00]: It's even meaningless to me.

161
00:17:28,038 --> 00:17:28,938
[SPEAKER_00]: I know it's meaningless.

162
00:17:29,578 --> 00:17:33,420
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no amount of self-induced conviction that will make me believe.

163
00:17:33,480 --> 00:17:34,180
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it isn't mine.

164
00:17:34,240 --> 00:17:35,081
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be mine.

165
00:17:35,981 --> 00:17:37,462
[SPEAKER_00]: This planet Earth is mine.

166
00:17:38,002 --> 00:17:38,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

167
00:17:39,643 --> 00:17:41,743
[SPEAKER_00]: But you know that that statement is hollow.

168
00:17:42,244 --> 00:17:45,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not more hollow than saying that this body is mine.

169
00:17:45,985 --> 00:17:46,265
[SPEAKER_00]: It isn't.

170
00:17:46,425 --> 00:17:47,606
[SPEAKER_00]: It's absolutely on the same footing.

171
00:17:50,570 --> 00:17:59,552
[SPEAKER_00]: And if that is discerned, then, yeah, it will be equally meaningless for you to think that this body, this heart, this lungs, this... these senses are mine.

172
00:18:00,872 --> 00:18:02,893
[SPEAKER_00]: And then there will be no problem left for you to deal with.

173
00:18:04,533 --> 00:18:10,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have surmounted, let go of, extinguished the basis of all problem.

174
00:18:11,795 --> 00:18:15,296
[SPEAKER_00]: So you don't go around dealing with every single problem individually.

175
00:18:16,016 --> 00:18:19,357
[SPEAKER_00]: You just uproot the basis for any problem to arise.

